On $[0, 1]$ the upper and lower integrals of the Dirichlet function are $I^* (f) = 1$ and $I_* (f) = 0$, so $f$ is not Riemann integrable.

This contrasts with the Thomae function, which is also nonzero at infinitely many, indeed dense many points, yet is integrable: there the values at the bad points tend to $0$, so the upper sums can be made arbitrarily small.
